<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sinhala-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinhala language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinhala is the Indo-Aryan language of the Sinhalese people and one of the official languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, written in its own script. Descended from the speech of settlers from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 1st millennium BCE, it developed apart from related languages and absorbed influences from Pali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Tamil, and later from Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English. Its literature goes back more than a thousand years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinhala-language.jpg" alt="Sinhala language" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RomeshD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigiriya-graffiti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
